    "result_text": "The full suite is green: **52 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors, BUILD SUCCESS**.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the disposable-domain email blocklist as an OfficeFloor function-injection step, following the project's established endpoint patterns.\n\n**New files:**\n- `function/owner/CheckEmailDomainAllowed.java` \u2014 reads the validated body via `@Val OwnerFieldsDto`, extracts the domain after the final `@`, and throws `DisposableEmailDomainException` if it's in the blocklist (`mailinator.com`, `tempmail.com`, `guerrillamail.com`). It runs after `NormalizeOwnerEmail`, so the email is already present and lower-cased; null/blank emails (email is optional) are skipped.\n- `escalation/DisposableEmailDomainException.java` \u2014 a checked exception carrying the offending address.\n- `escalation/DisposableEmailDomainHandler.java` \u2014 maps it to a 400 `ProblemDetail` naming `email` in the `errors` array, mirroring the existing `InvalidEmailHandler`.\n- `officefloor/escalation/...DisposableEmailDomainException.yml` \u2014 wires the handler.\n\n**Wiring:** inserted a `checkEmailDomain` step (governed by `transaction`) right after `normalizeEmail` in both `owners.POST.yml` and `owners/{ownerId}.PUT.yml`, so the rule applies wherever an owner email is accepted.\n\nI gave this its own exception rather than reusing `InvalidEmailException` because a blocklisted domain is a policy rejection distinct from syntactic invalidity \u2014 consistent with how each other owner rule in this codebase has a dedicated exception/handler pair.",
